{{ ansible_managed | comment }}
#
# Shared secrets authenticating zone transfers to the secondaries
# (TSIG, RFC 8945). The secondary uses one to verify that the zone it
# received really came from this host and was not altered in transit,
# and this host uses it to refuse a transfer to anyone who cannot
# prove they hold the same secret.
#
# One key per zone, not one per server. Hetzner's console derives the
# key name from the zone and this host's address ("<zone>.<primary-ip>."),
# so the names differ whether or not the secrets do - and keeping the
# secrets separate as well means rotating one zone's key never touches
# another's.
#
# This is NOT DNSSEC, which is a separate matter still open. TSIG
# authenticates the hop between these two servers with a shared secret
# and leaves nothing behind in the zone; DNSSEC signs the zone data
# itself with public-key cryptography so that any validating resolver
# anywhere can check an individual answer. A TSIG-protected transfer
# of an unsigned zone is still unverifiable to the outside world, and
# a DNSSEC-signed zone transferred without TSIG is still fully
# verifiable by resolvers. Neither substitutes for the other.
#
# Kept out of nsd.conf so that the secrets do not show up in a --diff,
# which the drift check runs routinely. Both ends must agree on the key
# *name* as well as the secret; the name travels in the clear as part
# of the transfer.
{# Deduplicated by name: NSD rejects the config outright if the same
   key name is defined twice, which two zones sharing a key would
   otherwise cause. Sharing one is not the intent here, but a config
   that fails to parse is a worse way to find that out. #}
{% set emitted = [] %}
{% for z in nsd.zones if z.tsig.secret %}
{% if z.tsig.name not in emitted %}
{% set _ = emitted.append(z.tsig.name) %}

# {{ z.name }}
key:
  name: "{{ z.tsig.name }}"
  algorithm: {{ z.tsig.algorithm }}
  secret: "{{ z.tsig.secret }}"
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
